5. Why FD tracking matters to the forensic objective
Now we can evaluate FD tracking without turning it into a separate project.

An FD is a process-visible reference to an open file description. The open file description refers to the kernel file object.

The distinction is essential for two of our original event families.

For memfd:
Process
  |
  | memfd_create()
  v
Returned FD
  |
  v
struct file
  |
  ├── write()
  ├── dup()
  ├── inherited through fork()
  └── execveat()


Without the FD-to-object relationship, we cannot reliably establish which memfd was written to or executed.

For unlink:
Process A
  |
  | open()
  v
FD 5 ───────────────┐
                    |
Process B           v
  |              File object
  | unlink()        |
  v                 |
Directory entry removed
                    |
                    v
             FD 5 still references
             the open file description


The pathname can disappear while the underlying file remains accessible.

This leads to an important architectural conclusion:

FD tracking is required selectively to establish object provenance and lifetime. It is not an independent objective of EphimTrace.

We should also distinguish the relevant identities:
- FD number: reusable integer, meaningful within a descriptor table.
- Descriptor table: may be shared by multiple tasks.
- struct file: represents an open file description.
- Inode/device: identifies the underlying filesystem object, where applicable.
Two separate open() calls on the same inode may produce different open file descriptions. Two duplicated descriptors may reference the same open file description.

Therefore, inode identity cannot replace open-file identity.
